  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nOver the last 6 hours, Flash Flood Warnings have been converted\nto Flood Warnings. Although conditions are currently clear and\ncalm, creeks and streams, particularly near Red Bank Creek\nnortheast of Pittsburgh, may remain elevated until dawn due to\nearlier heavy rainfall (3-4 inches). Red Bank Creek near\nSt.Charles may need to placed on a River Flood Warning in the\nnext 3-6 hours if it crests above 17ft. Please continue to\nrespect all road closures until water levels recede. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nMachine learning guidance has remained consistent in signaling \nthat a low pressure system tracking across the Great Lakes on \nTuesday will bring another threat of severe storms to the \nregion. The Storm Prediction Center (SPC) has outlined the \nentire forecast domain in a 15% severe weather risk. Damaging \nwind gusts and tornadoes appear to the be the primary hazard as \nstrong wind shear accompanies this large-scale system. \n\nIn addition to the severe weather threat, there is also the\npotential for training showers and storms with a potential\nmesoscale convection system (MCS) across the Ohio River Valley.\nRainfall totals could range between 1.5-2 inches in a short \nperiod of time and result in flash flooding.",
